TRPV1 activation measured by CGRP output
CGRP levels in SF (n = 44 patients; 21 high pain and 23 low pain), measured by ELISA, served as an indirect marker of peripheral TRPV1 activity, as TRPV1 activation on sensory nerves stimulates CGRP release (Figure
2E
). A statistically significant, albeit weak, negative linear correlation was observed between SF CGRP concentrations and KOOS pain scores (where lower KOOS scores indicate higher pain) (r(42) = -0.303, p = 0.045). This suggests that higher CGRP levels were associated with higher self-reported pain. When comparing the means between the KOOS pain quartiles, the difference in CGRP levels were nearly significant, with higher mean concentration in the high-pain group (low pain: 24.7 ± 16.2 pg/mL versus high pain: 40.7 ± 23.5 pg/mL; p = 0.0547) (Figure
2E
).
2-AG and AEA levels in SF
LC-MS/MS analysis of endocannabinoids in SF revealed significantly higher levels of 2-AG in patients in the high-pain group (20.9 ± 10.3 ng/mL) compared to the low-pain group (14.9 ± 7.1 ng/mL) (p = 0.0358) (Figure
3A
). Conversely, similar concentrations of AEA were observed between high (0.99 ± 0.05 ng/mL) and low (0.94 ± 0.04 ng/mL) pain groups (p > 0.05) (Figure
3A
).
